# Accessibility (a11y) and Inclusion

## Context

- Ensures all user-facing screens and components in Expo applications meet basic accessibility standards.
- Applies to interactions, visual elements, and markup structure within React Native components.

## Requirements

### Core Accessibility Principles

- Follow WCAG 2.1 guidelines at minimum AA compliance level
- Design with accessibility in mind from the start
- Use native semantics when possible, add ARIA when necessary
  - Using standard React Native accessibility props first
  - Only adding complex accessibility patterns when standard props are insufficient
  - Never assuming any accessibility feature is automatically provided
- Test with real assistive technologies:
  - Screen readers (VoiceOver, TalkBack)
  - Switch controls
  - Voice commands

<required>

1. Semantic Roles: React Native components do NOT automatically provide semantic roles equivalent to web HTML elements. Always explicitly set appropriate `accessibilityRole` properties.
2. Accessibility Labels:
   - Let text speak for itself: If a component contains Text elements, React Native automatically uses that text as the accessibility label
   - Only add explicit accessibilityLabel when:
     a) The element contains NO text (e.g., an icon-only button)
     b) An image conveys meaning that isn't already communicated by text
   - Avoid redundant labels: Never add `accessibilityLabel` to elements that already contain descriptive text

</required>

### Semantic Structure

- Use semantic components:
  - Proper heading hierarchy
  - Semantic sectioning (View, Text, etc.)
  - Meaningful component naming
- Implement proper focus management:
  - Focus indicators
  - Logical focus order
  - Focus trapping for modals
- Use consistent skip links or nav landmarks for clear page structure

### Interactive Elements

- Interactive component requirements:
  - `accessibilityRole` appropriate to their function
  - `accessibilityState` when representing states like checked, disabled, selected
  - `accessibilityHint` when additional context about the result of an action is
    helpful
- Ensure proper touch targets:
  - Minimum size of 44×44 points
  - Adequate spacing between elements
  - Proper hit slop configuration
- Implement keyboard navigation:
  - Tab navigation
  - Keyboard shortcuts
  - Proper keyboard focus indicators

### Content Accessibility

- Provide text alternatives:
  - Informative images need appropriate `accessibilityLabel` to convey their meaning
  - Captions for videos
  - Transcripts for audio
- Decorative images should be ignored by screen readers
- Ensure proper text contrast:
  - Minimum 4.5:1 for normal text
  - Minimum 3:1 for large text
  - Color not used as the only visual means of conveying information
- Ensure text scaling and font adjustments for accessibility

### Assistive Technology

- Implement proper `accessibilityLabel` and `accessibilityHint`
- Use `AccessibilityInfo` to announce changes in dynamic content to assistive technologies

### Testing Accessibility

- Implement automated accessibility testing:
  - Use accessibility linting rules
  - Integrate with Jest for component testing
  - Apply accessibility checkers in CI/CD
- Conduct manual accessibility reviews:
  - Screen reader testing
  - Keyboard navigation testing
  - Color contrast verification

## Examples

<example>

```tsx

import { Button, Text, View, AccessibilityInfo } from 'react-native';

// Icon-only button
<TouchableOpacity
  accessibilityRole="button"
  accessibilityLabel="Add to cart"
  accessibilityHint="Adds this item to your shopping cart"
  onPress={handleAddToCart}
>
  <Icon name="cart" />
</TouchableOpacity>

//  Status icon
<Icon name="check-circle" accessibilityRole=“image" accessibilityLabel="Completed" />

// Button with text: NO label needed
<TouchableOpacity accessibilityRole="button">
  <Text>Submit</Text>
</TouchableOpacity>

// Providing alt text
<Image source={require('./logo.png')} accessibilityLabel="Company Logo" />

// Managing focus
useEffect(() => {
  if (isOpen) {
    AccessibilityInfo.announceForAccessibility('Modal opened');
  }
}, [isOpen]);
```

</example>

<example type="invalid">

```tsx
// ❌ Using non-semantic elements
<TouchableOpacity onPress={handleSubmit}>Submit</TouchableOpacity>

// ❌ Redundant label
<TouchableOpacity accessibilityRole="button" accessibilityLabel="Submit form">
  <Text>Submit</Text>
</TouchableOpacity>

// ❌ Missing accessibility labels
<Image source={require('./logo.png')} />

// ❌ Not managing focus
useEffect(() => {
    // No focus management
}, [isOpen]);
```

</example>
